The posterior probability the settlement is settled is the sum of the two settled joint probabilities: \(0.035 + 0.001 = 0.036\).

Declaring “pending” has expected cost \(10 \times P(\text{not pending}) = 10 \times (0.036+0.007) = 0.43\), which is lower than escalating (4) or querying (at least cost 1 or 2 before any follow-up decision).

{"p_benign": 0.036, "action": "declare_pending"}

The posterior probability that the settlement is settled is **0.986**.  
The expected cost of declaring `settled` is \(30 \times (0.007+0.007)=0.42\). Any query costs at least 1, which already exceeds the maximum possible improvement from additional information, so the optimal next action is to declare settled.

{"p_benign": 0.986, "action": "declare_settled"}

The posterior probability that the settlement is settled is approximately \(0.956639\) (rounded to \(0.957\) as provided).

Immediate declaration of “settled” has expected cost \(60 \times (1-0.956639) \approx 2.60\). Escalating costs 4. A further `query_A` has a lower expected total cost even using conservative continuation assumptions, and it is cheaper/more informative than `query_V`.

{"p_benign": 0.956639, "action": "query_A"}

The posterior probability that the settlement is settled is 0.893.  
The next action minimizing expected total cost is to query Tool A again.

{"p_benign": 0.893, "action": "query_A"}

The posterior probability the service is currently healthy is approximately \(0.02135\).  
Given the very high posterior probability that the service is down, declaring “down” has the lowest expected immediate cost: about \((0.02135+0.00378)\times 10 \approx 0.251\). Any query costs at least 1, which already exceeds the maximum possible improvement over this declaration cost.

{"p_benign": 0.0213477205, "action": "declare_down"}

